# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Environment variables paddock reads
#
# These are not config keys -- there is nowhere in this file to set them -- so
# they are listed here because otherwise the only record of them is the source.
# Anything under `[models.<name>.env]` or `[benchmarks.<name>]` above is a
# better place to pin behaviour for a run; these are the escape hatches.
#
#   PADDOCK_CONFIG          use a different site config than ./paddock.toml
#   PADDOCK_BASE_PORT       first port a run scans from. Two concurrent runs
#                           both scanning from 9000 collide; give each its own.
#   PADDOCK_FFMPEG          ffmpeg binary, when it is not on PATH. Also settable
#                           as [benchmarks.neuroncap] ffmpeg.
#   PADDOCK_FFPROBE         ffprobe binary. Unset, paddock looks beside
#                           PADDOCK_FFMPEG first and then on PATH -- a site that
#                           pointed PADDOCK_FFMPEG into another project's venv
#                           has both there.
#   PADDOCK_FONT            TrueType font for the comma2k19 overlay's captions,
#                           for a site whose Pillow ships no usable bundled one.
#
# openpilot's node, all read inside its own interpreter. Set them under
# [models.openpilot.env] rather than exporting them, so they are on the record
# in run.json:
#
#   PADDOCK_OPENPILOT_BACKEND       "tinygrad" or "onnx". Unset, the node picks
#                                   by trying the import, and records which it
#                                   got under provenance.policy.
#   PADDOCK_OPENPILOT_MODEL         "small" (driving_supercombo, the default)
#                                   or "big" (big_driving_supercombo)
#   PADDOCK_OPENPILOT_CUDA_MODEL    path to the CUDA-compiled tinygrad pickle
#   PADDOCK_OPENPILOT_ONNX          path to a *combined* ONNX graph. Set, it is
#                                   the only candidate, so a typo fails loudly
#                                   instead of falling back to the split pair.
#   PADDOCK_OPENPILOT_MODE          "experimental" (the default: openpilot's
#                                   end-to-end longitudinal head) or ""
#   PADDOCK_OPENPILOT_NATIVE_LONG   "1" to let openpilot's own longitudinal
#                                   planner set speed instead of the harness
#   PADDOCK_OPENPILOT_RESUME_S      seconds stopped with no lead before the
#                                   driver-resume rule pulls away; "0" disables
#   PADDOCK_OPENPILOT_THREADS       onnxruntime thread count (default 8)
#   PADDOCK_OPENPILOT_DESIRE        "off" (the default: no desire, as every
#                                   run before it), "turn" or "lane_change":
#                                   which desire a LEFT/RIGHT route command is
#                                   sent as. See docs/models/openpilot-guide.md.
#   PADDOCK_OPENPILOT_HEIGHT_SCALE  the camera's height over the road divided
#                                   by openpilot's 1.22 m, to show the model
#                                   the road as its own camera would have seen
#                                   it. "1" (the default) is openpilot's own
#                                   warp. A measurement, not a correction: see
#                                   docs/models/openpilot-guide.md.
#   PADDOCK_OPENPILOT_PITCH_DEG     degrees the model's view is tilted over
#                                   the camera's, positive up. "0" (the
#                                   default) is openpilot's own warp.
#   PADDOCK_OPENPILOT_FOV_DEG       warp the views from a cut of this many
#                                   degrees across, resampled to comma's
#                                   1164x874, instead of the frame as recorded.
#                                   "0" (the default) is no cut. Needs
#                                   PADDOCK_OPENPILOT_BACKEND = "onnx". Both:
#                                   see docs/models/openpilot-guide.md. Both
#                                   are set for one camera rig, so set them in
#                                   a pairing table (above) for the benchmark
#                                   whose rig they were measured on.
